{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"SRC_001's calibration signature is not a valid way to distinguish a genuine reliability gain from mere checklist compliance within a single evaluation. A panel can exhibit monotonic confidence/agreement that is perfectly correlated with evidence clarity even when merely mechanically following the checklist; in fact, a well-specified checklist will produce higher agreement on clear cases and lower agreement on ambiguous ones as a mechanical byproduct. This signature tracks checklist determinacy, not genuine reliability. Counterexample: a panel blindly following a flawed checklist will show monotonic confidence based solely on item-match count—producing the exact calibration curve described—without any genuine reliability gain. The target also conflates 'frequentist justification' with reliability, ignoring that a checklist can be reproducibly wrong.","counterexample":[{"scenario":"Checklist says: if 3 of 4 binary items are present, judge YES.","panel_behavior":"Panel counts items mechanically, producing unanimous high-confidence YES when 4/4 items present, split low-confidence when 2/4.","calibration_observed":"Monotonic confidence-evidence relationship exactly as SRC_001 predicts.","actual_reliability":"No genuine gain; checklist items are spurious and the panel is reliably wrong."}],"target_alias":"SRC_001"},{"attack":false,"target_alias":"SRC_002"},{"attack":true,"case":"SRC_003's signature—'variance reduction across panelists on evidence sub-items' as the mark of genuine gain vs. 'high final-answer agreement with high sub-item variance' as mere compliance—is theoretically unstable. A panel of trained non-experts might show LOW sub-item variance simply because they all identically misunderstand or mechanically apply the checklist items, which is pure compliance, not genuine gain. Conversely, high sub-item variance can coexist with genuine reliability if panelists reach the correct answer through different valid evidence-weighting paths. Counterexample: a panel independently misreads a checklist item identically (low variance) yet is systematically wrong on edge cases; per SRC_003's test this looks like genuine gain, but the panel has no reliability beyond the checklist's flawed encoding.","counterexample":[{"scenario":"Checklist item 3 asks 'Is the variance explained?' Panelists uniformly interpret this as 'Is variance mentioned?' and answer identically (low sub-item variance).","observed_signature":"Low sub-item variance per SRC_003 → classified as genuine gain.","actual_reliability":"No genuine gain; uniform misinterpretation is pure compliance that fails on edge cases requiring true variance assessment."}],"target_alias":"SRC_003"}]}